[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR RIP routing on router3 (the transit router between the WAN core and the 172.18.50/51 site).

Mechanism: Forward traffic towards 172.18.51.0/24 can reach router6 via r0→r1→r2→r3 (router2 has static routes to 172.18.50/51 via 192.168.100.2). However, the return path breaks at router3 because it does not participate in RIP and has no routes to the rest of the network subnets. This creates asymmetric routing: requests get there, replies die at router3, making the “other site” appear to come and go depending on direction/host.

Localization evidence:
- router3 RIP: “router rip” has only “version 2” and no network statements; “show ip rip” is empty. Its kernel table only has connected and static to 172.18.50/51 via 10.50.1.2 (router6), with no routes to 172.16.10.0/24 or other core nets.
- From router6, pinging a core host fails with an ICMP from router3: “From 10.50.1.1 Destination Net Unreachable” for 172.16.10.4, proving router3 lacks a return route.
- From router0, traceroute to 172.18.51.16 reaches r1 (10.16.1.2) and r2 (10.20.1.2) then stops, consistent with replies being blackholed at router3.

Root cause: RIP is not enabled on router3’s upstream/downstream interfaces, so router3 neither learns nor advertises routes.

Corrective fix: On router3, enable RIP on its connected networks:
- router rip
  version 2
  network 10.50.1.0/24
  network 192.168.100.0/24
  network 172.25.1.0/24
Optionally, on router6 add network 172.18.0.0/16 to advertise LANs. Afterward, verify “show ip rip” has learned routes and end-to-end pings between 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 succeed. === END ===